title BUHR Journey 3 exploration — MCP-in-Bron, the door opened
prompt recur BUHR crux-first, double-seat new visions, send each increment, stop at custody gates
think Journey 2's crux is proven across two witnessed rungs (render + styled paint, editor and view share one graph). The next itinerary item is Journey 3 (MCP-in-Bron), which is genuinely greenfield — a new domain — so a grounded design pass rightly precedes code.
think Grounded the vision in real surfaces, not invention: Bron notation (yonder/20260621-063912), the bounded request/response pattern already proven (mandate/serve.rye), and the per-key infuse cascade (brix/infuse.rye) a manifest can inherit defaults through. Confirmed the sibling J2 exploration already names MCP-in-Bron, so naming MCP/JSON here follows the established BUHR precedent rather than breaking the active-designing silo fresh.
think Ordered Lindy-first crux-first: the durable artifact is the descriptor SHAPE (format mcp-tool-v1 manifest · mcp-call-v1 / mcp-result-v1 envelope), read far more often than any tile runs; the crux is making that shape bounded, value-model-honest, and round-trippable, with an unknown field refused not ignored. The Bron<->JSON tiles are named horizon — mechanical once the shape is fixed.
think Named a concrete first door: pinned Bron descriptors for one real tool (the Journey 2 Mantra browser is the natural first tool to describe), a bounded Rye reader that parses a call envelope and renders a result back to Bron with named errors, and a witness proving the round trip deterministic on metal. Agent-doable, no custody, no new transport.
obs Docs lint GREEN (advisory only, no link breaks on the new doc). This is a design-in-motion, not a build — nothing is implemented by it; it sets the next code rung's target.
file active-designing/20260812-111800_buhr-journey3-mcp-in-bron-exploration.md the Journey 3 vision — MCP-in-Bron descriptors, the crux (descriptor before tile), and the first door
recommend check-in (Claude) Journey 3 opens a new domain (a protocol descriptor spoken in the owned notation) — the descriptor shape (mcp-tool-v1 field grammar, unknown-field refusal, the first tool to describe) is a design ruling worth confirming before the first code rung
